  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & ={X}^{2}+X-4 \\\\  & ={X}^{2}+X-4I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(B)={B}^{2}+B-4I.$\n\nLet's calculate ${B}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(B):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B) & ={B}^{2}+B-4I \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+0-4 & 6+2-0 \\\\ -3+(-1)-0 & 7+3-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
